Subject: Re: LR test in DIF vs DRF
Message-ID: <bHaQ0BEaNK0.Z8UV2ogkAQAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <bHaQ0BEaNK0.mpfEKjUkAQAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
References: <bHaQ0BEaNK0.VdDgpjAiAQAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com> <bHaQ0BEaNK0.mpfEKjUkAQAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/bHaQ0BEaNK0/m/Z8UV2ogkAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Yes, this sounds like it was a df issue then. There's probably only a few parameters that are difference across the groups, so there are many unnecessary df being used in the LR test (e.g., if the first two intercepts were different only then testing the remaining third, fourth, fifth, ...., only decreases power). You could try isolating different sets of item 7's parameters to determine where the differences are, which is very analogous in the context of ANOVA analyses to run post-hoc tests to determine where the effects lay. In any event, I think this is one of those cases where the DRF statistics are probably outperforming traditional likelihood-based parametric DIF tests.

Phil

On Fri, Nov 8, 2019 at 11:48 AM Keri J. S. Brady <keri.j....@gmail.com> wrote:
> Phil, thanks so much! That would make total sense re: dfs being the issue (particularly because I'm doing a joint test of all parameters in the LR test). The scale is an 8 item polytomous scale (7 response categories for 7 items; 13 response categories for the other remaining item). Here is my code. (Note that I used the drop sequential approach prior to the LR test below to identify my anchors - all the items w/ no DIF [which was all items except item x7] were added to the unconstrained model below as anchors). Thank you, Phil! - Keri
